--TEST--
A doc comment carries as many `@see` and `@example` lines as the declaration has cross-references and examples, and every one of them resolves on its own — `rule:tooling/doc-comment-tags-are-see-and-example`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:tooling/doc-comment-tags-are-see-and-example`: both tags repeat. The
// four lines under the class's prose are one block, each is resolved by
// itself, and the order they are written in is the order they are read back
// in — a card carries `see` and `example` as lists rather than as one target
// each.
/// A till, and the money it has taken.
///
/// @see Till::charge
/// @see Till::$taken
/// @example examples/charge.nvs
/// @example examples/refund.nvs
class Till {
    /// Everything this till has taken, in cents.
    public int $taken;

    /// Builds a till that has taken nothing yet.
    public function constructor() {
        $this->taken = 0;
    }

    /// Takes `$cents`, and answers the running total.
    ///
    /// A member repeats them on the same terms the class does, and mixes them
    /// in whatever order reads best: nothing requires the `@see` lines to come
    /// before the `@example` ones.
    ///
    /// @example examples/charge.nvs
    /// @see Till::$taken
    /// @example examples/refund.nvs
    public function charge(int $cents): int {
        $this->taken = $this->taken + $cents;
        return $this->taken;
    }
}

var $till = new Till();
echo $till->charge(150), "\n";
echo $till->charge(50), "\n";
--FILE examples/charge.nvs--
<?nvs
// One of the two files the `@example` lines name. Both have to exist and both
// have to sit in a directory the corpus walks, so a second example is held to
// the same standard as the first.
echo "a charge\n";
--FILE examples/refund.nvs--
<?nvs
// The second one, which a check that stopped at a declaration's first
// `@example` would never look for.
echo "a refund\n";
--EXPECT--
150
200
